.TH TOGLER 1 "__DATE__" "__VERSION__" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
togler \- toggle application visibility on GNOME
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B togler
[\fB-t\fR|\fB--toggle\fR \fI<app-name>\fR [\fI<launch-command>\fR]]
.br
.B togler
[\fB-l\fR|\fB--list\fR]
.br
.B togler
[\fB-e\fR|\fB--edit\fR]
.br
.B togler
[\fB-v\fR|\fB--version\fR]
.br
.B togler
[\fB-h\fR|\fB--help\fR]

.SH DESCRIPTION
Togler is a command-line utility for toggling application window visibility on GNOME. It can hide/show windows or launch applications if they are not running. Togler handles differences between Wayland and X11 WM_CLASS values through a configuration file.

When toggling an application:
.IP \(bu 2
If the window is focused, it will be minimized
.IP \(bu 2
If the window exists but is not focused, it will be activated
.IP \(bu 2
If the window does not exist, the application will be launched

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -t ", " --toggle " <app-name> [launch-command]"
Toggle the visibility of the specified application. The app-name should match an entry in the configuration file. An optional launch-command can override the configured command.
.TP
.BR -l ", " --list
List all configured applications with their Wayland and X11 WM_CLASS values.
.TP
.BR -e ", " --edit
Open the configuration file in your default editor (set by $EDITOR, defaults to nano).
.TP
.BR -v ", " --version
Display version information and exit.
.TP
.BR -h ", " --help
Display help message with usage information and exit.

.SH CONFIGURATION
Togler uses a configuration file to map application names to their WM_CLASS values for both Wayland and X11 sessions. The configuration file is located at:

.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/togler/apps.conf
.br
(typically ~/.config/togler/apps.conf)

.SS Configuration Format
Each line in the configuration file follows this format:

.B app_name|wayland_class|x11_class|launch_command

Lines starting with # are treated as comments and ignored.

.SS Default Configuration
On first run, togler creates a default configuration with common applications:
.IP \(bu 2
chrome - Google Chrome/Chromium
.IP \(bu 2
firefox - Mozilla Firefox
.IP \(bu 2
kitty - Kitty terminal
.IP \(bu 2
terminal - GNOME Terminal
.IP \(bu 2
vscode - Visual Studio Code
.IP \(bu 2
And more...

.SS Adding Custom Applications
To add a custom application, edit the configuration file:

.B togler --edit

Then add a line with the format described above. To find the WM_CLASS of a running application, you can use:
.IP \(bu 2
On X11: xprop WM_CLASS (then click the window)
.IP \(bu 2
On Wayland: Use GNOME's Looking Glass (Alt+F2, type 'lg', then inspect windows)

.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B togler -t chrome
Toggle the Google Chrome window. If Chrome is focused, minimize it. If it's open but not focused, bring it to focus. If it's not running, launch it.
.TP
.B togler -t terminal
Toggle the GNOME Terminal window.
.TP
.B togler -t kitty
Toggle the Kitty terminal window.
.TP
.B togler -t myapp /opt/myapp/bin/myapp
Toggle a custom application with a specific launch command.
.TP
.B togler --list
Display all configured applications.
.TP
.B togler --edit
Open the configuration file for editing.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_SESSION_TYPE
Used to determine whether the session is running Wayland or X11. Togler automatically selects the appropriate WM_CLASS value based on this.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Used to locate the configuration directory. Defaults to ~/.config if not set.
.TP
.B EDITOR
Used when editing the configuration file with --edit. Defaults to nano if not set.

.SH DEPENDENCIES
Togler requires the following:
.TP
.B GNOME Shell
The desktop environment where togler operates.
.TP
.B Togler GNOME Extension
A companion GNOME Shell extension that provides D-Bus interface for window toggling. Must be installed and enabled.
.TP
.B gdbus
Command-line tool for D-Bus communication (part of GLib).

.SH FILES
.TP
.B ~/.config/togler/apps.conf
User configuration file containing application mappings.

.SH SEE ALSO
gsettings(1), gnome-shell(1), gdbus(1)

.SH AUTHOR
Written by Caesar (https://github.com/caesar003)

.SH BUGS
Report bugs at: https://github.com/caesar003/togler/issues

.SH NOTES
The companion GNOME Shell extension must be installed and enabled for togler to function. The extension provides the D-Bus interface that togler uses to communicate with GNOME Shell.
